---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import {repoUrl,syllabusUrl} from '../data/curriculum';
---
<Base title="About & corrections" active="about"><div class="page-heading"><span class="eyebrow">AN OPEN NOTEBOOK</span><h1>For the questions<br/><em>you need to solve.</em></h1><p>MathNotes is a growing independent resource for students learning CIE A Level Further Mathematics.</p></div><div class="about-content"><h2>Built from a real study collection</h2><p>The project began with a collection of lesson notes, topic worksheets and answer compilations. The collection contains 50 PDFs, representing 42 unique documents. Its topics provide the starting structure for this site.</p><p>Each guide follows the numbered question types in the source notes. Textbook and worksheet examples are shortened for revision and shown with concise worked solutions. Examples from formula-only annotations are identified separately. Each example records the source filename and note page.</p><h2>What is ready</h2><p>The site includes all 14 topics represented in the current notes: five in FP1, four in FP2 and five in Further Statistics. The collection contains 78 numbered question types, each with a method, formula and worked example, plus 48 separate original practice questions. Topics without supplied notes are omitted from navigation and the topic map.</p><p>The reference baseline is the <a href={syllabusUrl}>2026–2027 Cambridge 9231 syllabus</a>. This project is independent of Cambridge International and is not endorsed by it. Availability of a guide does not imply full syllabus coverage.</p><h2>Accuracy and review</h2><p>These notes are an initial editorial release. Worked answers have been checked during development; an independent teacher review has not yet been recorded. If something looks wrong, sharing the section and your working helps improve the next revision.</p><h2>Help make it clearer</h2><p>Report a mathematical error, unclear explanation or broken link through GitHub Issues. Include the page, the relevant line or question, and a proposed correction where possible. Submitting an issue uses your GitHub account.</p><a class="button primary" href={`${repoUrl}/issues/new?title=Correction%20or%20suggestion&body=Page%3A%0A%0ASection%20or%20question%3A%0A%0AProposed%20correction%20and%20reason%3A%0A`}>Suggest a correction <Icon name="external" size={15}/></a><h2>A small note on privacy</h2><p>No account is needed to read or practise. Marking a topic as studied stores that choice in your browser on this device. This release has no analytics, advertising or account database. Search runs in your browser, and fonts are served with the site.</p><p>Built and maintained by Justin Wang. Source code and contribution instructions are available on <a href={repoUrl}>GitHub</a>.</p></div></Base>
